1. Character Device Drivers (User-Kernel Communication)
Understanding how the user space communicates with the kernel via device files (like /dev/mydevice) is fundamental.
Key Concept: Implementing file_operations (read, write, open, release).
c
#include <linux/fs.h>
#include <linux/uaccess.h> // for copy_to_user

static int my_open(struct inode *i, struct file *f) { return 0; }

// Copying data from Kernel space to User space
static ssize_t my_read(struct file *f, char __user *buf, size_t len, loff_t *off) {
    char *msg = "Kernel Data";
    if (copy_to_user(buf, msg, 11)) return -EFAULT;
    return 11;
}

static struct file_operations fops = {
    .owner = THIS_MODULE,
    .open = my_open,
    .read = my_read,
};
Use code with caution.

2. Concurrency and Synchronization
The kernel is highly parallel. You must prevent Race Conditions using Mutexes or Spinlocks. By 2026, understanding Lockless programming (like RCU) is a major bonus.
Key Concept: Protecting shared data.
c
#include <linux/mutex.h>

struct mutex my_lock;
int shared_resource = 0;

void safe_update(void) {
    mutex_lock(&my_lock);
    shared_resource++;  // Critical Section
    mutex_unlock(&my_lock);
}
Use code with caution.

3. Interrupt Handling & Bottom Halves
Hardware triggers interrupts (IRQ). Since you can't "sleep" inside a hard interrupt, you must use Tasklets or Workqueues.
Key Concept: Keeping the ISR (Interrupt Service Routine) fast.
c
#include <linux/interrupt.h>

// This runs in "Interrupt Context" (must be fast!)
irqreturn_t irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id) {
    printk(KERN_INFO "Interrupt Occurred!\n");
    return IRQ_HANDLED; 
}

// Registering the interrupt (e.g., for IRQ 1 - keyboard)
// request_irq(1, irq_handler, IRQF_SHARED, "my_handler", (void *)(irq_handler));
Use code with caution.

4. Memory Management (kmalloc vs. vmalloc)
You must understand how the kernel allocates memory and handles virtual vs. physical mapping.
kmalloc(): Allocates physically contiguous memory (best for small buffers/DMA).
vmalloc(): Allocates virtually contiguous memory (better for large buffers).
c
#include <linux/slab.h> // for kmalloc

char *ptr;
ptr = kmalloc(1024, GFP_KERNEL); // GFP_KERNEL may sleep
if (!ptr) return -ENOMEM;

kfree(ptr);
Use code with caution.

